  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 700 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity persists today across the\nsouth, along with isolated to scattered showers and storms\narea-wide late in the day and into tonight.\n\nEarly morning WX analysis indicates a W-NW flow aloft with the\nBermuda high now getting shunted farther south to the Gulf coast\nand Bahamas. The latest radar indicates a decaying MCS affecting\nthe northern 1/2 of the area, with a series of trailing systems\nupstream over OH/WV and another farther NW IA/IL. \n\nWith the airmass overturned from yesterday's widespread convection\nacross VA/MD, today's SVR potential is highly uncertain. Debris\nclouds are likely to persist across at least the northern 1/2 \nof the CWA through late morning/early aftn which will limit or \nat least delay instability. The CAMs, which generally did poorly\nyesterday show varying solutions for this aftn/evening- the 06Z\nRRFS has backed off with an organized line of storms through \nthe CWA from late aftn into the early evening, now suggesting\nthe main event could be this evening/tonight (which matches\ncloser to the next shortwave aloft). SPC has most of the area \nin a Marginal SVR risk, primarily for damaging wind gusts, as \nshear is decent for August (25-30 kt) with a Slight Risk just \nmaking it into our far NW. The SPC discussion does note the high\nuncertainty given the current MCS and how this might affect \naftn conditions. In addition, a Marginal ERO is in effect for \nmost of the region (with a Slight nudging into the far NW). The \nsfc winds are a bit more from the W-SW today, and given this, \ndecided to hoist a small Heat Advisory for the interior NE NC \nzones along the Albemarle sound where sfc dew pts should be \nseveral degrees higher, along with highs into the mid 90s, \nleading to heat indices of 105+ for a few hrs. Elsewhere, highs \nwill generally be in the lower 90s (upper 80s/around 90F far N \nwith heat indices in the upper 90s to lower 100s). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled pattern continues through the \nremainder of the week, trending to a cooler and somewhat drier \nstart to the weekend. However, uncertainty remains high.\n\nWNW flow aloft continues Wed through the remainder of the week \nas a ridge aloft is expected to continue through late in the \nweek as an upper level ridge builds across the central CONUS, \nand eventually shifts E-SE across the deep south. Meanwhile, a \ntrough lingers across New England and eastern Canada. Expect an \nactive pattern with the daily potential for isolated to \nscattered storms each afternoon/evening. \n\nSPC has maintained a marginal risk (level 1/5) for severe storms \nacross southern portions of the FA Wed. However, confidence \ncontinues to remain low given uncertainty in aforementioned MCS\ntiming and impacts. For now, have largely kept NBM PoPs \n(30-35% SW to 15-25% NE) to account for the potential for \nisolated to scattered storms. Beyond Wed, the pattern remains \nsimilar with mostly only a slight chance (15-25%) for a few \nisolated showers or storms on Thu (and only general thunder \nrisk) with a better chance on Fri (30-40% PoPs). Outside of \nconvection, temps remain near the warm side of normal with highs\nin the upper 80s to lower 90s Wed and Thu and upper 80s to \nlower 90s Fri.",
